xnu 0415: a frame of a UBWC surface that goes the copy's way is decompressed, not read as rows

present()'s scaled copy read every surface as linear rows from its offset. A '&BGA' framebuffer of the pool is in the
kernel's UBWC layout (flag buffer first, TILE6_3 data after it), so each of its frames that was copied instead of
scanned out put the flag buffer and the compressed blocks on the panel: lines and pieces of the picture mixed with the
previous frame. That happens whenever a surface of the window server's ring cannot be scanned out (0354, "frames
copied for them": 1337 in 17 minutes on 2026-11-19, with the pool full), when a surface is swapped again while on the
panel, and when the scanout is turned off.

The copy now reads such a surface as Turnip's r2d_src reads a UBWC view: SP_PS_2D_SRC_INFO with TILE6_3, FLAGS and
WZYX, SP_PS_2D_SRC_FLAGS with the flag buffer and its pitch, the whole rectangle in GRAS_A2D_SRC_* (a tiled source is
not cropped by its address), and the destination in WXYZ so that the flip buffer holds B,G,R,A as the plane scans it.
The unscaled copy does not take a UBWC surface at all. kern.sweet_fb_ubwc_diag counts the decompressed copies.

The pool's census (kern.sweet_fb_scanout_diag) also says, for each slot, whether a program still has the surface (in
the table), the references the kernel holds on it, and how many GPU buffers are open over it and by which process: on
2026-11-19 three slots of eight belonged to framebuffers no program had any more, and the ring's new one went linear.
